message.content:
Fix these two issues in the Weekly Report / Progress page:

## 1. Replace "Upgrade" with "See More / See Less"
Find every instance of "Upgrade", "Upgrade to Pro", 
"Premium", "Unlock" anywhere in the app and replace with:

- Button text: "See More"
- When clicked: expand the content smoothly
- Show a "See Less" button to collapse it back
- No paywall, no modal, just smooth expand/collapse toggle
- Use framer-motion AnimatePresence for smooth animation:

const [expanded, setExpanded] = useState(false)

<button onClick={() => setExpanded(!expanded)}>
  {expanded ? 'See Less ↑' : 'See More ↓'}
</button>

<AnimatePresence>
  {expanded && (
    <motion.div
      initial={{ height: 0, opacity: 0 }}
      animate={{ height: 'auto', opacity: 1 }}
      exit={{ height: 0, opacity: 0 }}
      transition={{ duration: 0.3 }}
      style={{ overflow: 'hidden' }}
    >
      {/* expanded content */}
    </motion.div>
  )}
</AnimatePresence>

## 2. Fix PDF Download — Fit to 1-2 pages max
The PDF is getting cropped across many pages. Fix it:

const downloadPDF = async () => {
  setDownloading(true)

  const reportEl = document.getElementById('weekly-report')

  const canvas = await html2canvas(reportEl, {
    scale: 2,
    backgroundColor: '#fdf6f2',
    useCORS: true,
    windowWidth: 800,
    scrollY: 0
  })

  const imgData = canvas.toDataURL('image/png')
  const pdf = new jsPDF({
    orientation: 'portrait',
    unit: 'mm',
    format: 'a4'
  })

  const pageWidth = pdf.internal.pageSize.getWidth()   // 210mm
  const pageHeight = pdf.internal.pageSize.getHeight() // 297mm
  const margin = 10 // mm

  const usableWidth = pageWidth - margin * 2
  const imgHeightMm = (canvas.height * usableWidth) / canvas.width

  if (imgHeightMm <= pageHeight - margin * 2) {
    // Fits in 1 page — center it
    const yOffset = (pageHeight - imgHeightMm) / 2
    pdf.addImage(imgData, 'PNG', margin, yOffset, 
                 usableWidth, imgHeightMm)
  } else {
    // Split across 2 pages max — scale down to fit
    const scaleFactor = (pageHeight * 2 - margin * 4) / imgHeightMm
    const scaledWidth = usableWidth * scaleFactor
    const scaledHeight = imgHeightMm * scaleFactor
    const xOffset = (pageWidth - scaledWidth) / 2

    if (scaledHeight <= pageHeight - margin * 2) {
      pdf.addImage(imgData, 'PNG', xOffset, margin,
                   scaledWidth, scaledHeight)
    } else {
      // Page 1
      pdf.addImage(imgData, 'PNG', margin, margin,
                   usableWidth, imgHeightMm)
      // Page 2
      const page1Height = pageHeight - margin * 2
      pdf.addPage()
      pdf.addImage(imgData, 'PNG', margin, 
                   margin - page1Height,
                   usableWidth, imgHeightMm)
    }
  }

  pdf.save(`CalSnap-Report-${
    new Date().toISOString().slice(0,10)
  }.pdf`)
  setDownloading(false)
}

## 3. Make Report Content Compact for PDF
Before generating PDF, make the report card compact:
- Reduce font sizes slightly
- Reduce padding on all sections
- Hide the Download button itself during capture
  (add id="pdf-hide" and set display:none before capture)
- Restore after capture

## 4. Report card max width
Set the weekly-report div to:
max-width: 750px
margin: 0 auto
This ensures it renders cleanly for PDF capture.

